+++ b/SECURITY.md
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+# Daily Grind security audit
+
+The original page contained an inline stylesheet and one inline style attribute.
+It had no client-side JavaScript, event handlers, dynamic HTML insertion, forms,
+third-party scripts, or browser storage. The only external asset was an HTTPS
+Unsplash background image. The HTTP server provided no security headers.
+
+## Implemented protections
+
+- Moved all CSS into `/assets/styles.css`, preserving the existing design.
+- Enforced CSP with a deny-by-default resource policy. Only same-origin CSS and
+  images from `https://images.unsplash.com` are permitted. Scripts, inline CSS,
+  connections, plugins, framing, base URL changes, and form submissions are denied.
+- Required Trusted Types for script injection sinks and disallowed policy creation
+  in browsers that support it. No client script or permissive default policy is needed.
+- Added MIME sniffing protection, frame denial, no-referrer protection, and a
+  Permissions Policy disabling camera, microphone, location, payments, and USB.
+- Applied headers to all responses, including logout, CSS, and errors. Responses use
+  `no-store` so account-like routes and older HTML policies are not cached.
+- Used an explicit asset mapping, fixed URL parsing base, generic errors, and
+  GET/HEAD-only handling. No request path is used as a filesystem path.
+
+## Policy rollout
+
+`CSP_MODE=enforce` is the default. To stage the policy on a deployment, configure
+`CSP_MODE=report-only` in its environment. This emits the exact candidate policy
+as `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only`; it does **not** block CSP violations.
+Other security headers, including frame denial, remain active. Invalid CSP modes
+fail at startup rather than silently disabling the policy.
+
+Report-only violations appear in browser developer tools. There is no report
+collector or remote telemetry endpoint in this application. If centralized
+monitoring is required, provision a trusted collector and configure CSP reporting
+at the hosting layer before rollout. Do not treat report-only as enforcement.
+After assessing violations in staging, set `CSP_MODE=enforce` and redeploy. Future
+scripts, forms, fonts, or external assets require a deliberate policy review.
+
+Set `ENABLE_HSTS=true` only after the deployed origin reliably serves HTTPS.
+This starts HSTS with a one-day lifetime, without subdomain coverage or preloading.
+TLS termination, HTTP-to-HTTPS redirection, and any longer HSTS lifetime belong in
+the deployment configuration. Forwarded headers are not trusted to enable HSTS.
+The reverse proxy/CDN must preserve the application's security headers, serve
+the stylesheet with its CSS content type, and avoid conflicting CSP headers.
+A host that serves `index.html` directly must configure equivalent response
+headers; HTML alone cannot provide report-only policy or framing protection.
+
+## Validation and scope
+
+`npm run build` performs syntax checks and automated markup/policy and request
+handler checks. The checks call an isolated handler with mock requests/responses;
+they never import `server.js`, listen on a port, or use a browser. No browser or
+live deployment validation was performed.
+
+The existing `/logout` route only returns text; this application has no session
+or authentication implementation. These policies do not create authentication
+or CSRF protection. If sessions are introduced, implement logout as a protected
+state-changing operation and review cookie and cache behavior.
+
+References: [CSP](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP),
+[framing policy](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/frame-ancestors),
+[Trusted Types](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/require-trusted-types-for).
